WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.762
Every parcel leaves the corner shop the same way.

2
00:00:05.762 --> 00:00:09.074
Weigh it, choose a courier, book it, print the label.

3
00:00:09.074 --> 00:00:10.699
Only the choosing changes.

4
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.321
Most days, the shopkeeper picks the cheapest courier.

5
00:00:14.321 --> 00:00:16.452
For an urgent order, the fastest.

6
00:00:16.452 --> 00:00:18.195
And in heavy rain, the van.

7
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.887
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:21.887 --> 00:00:26.976
Pattern books draw Strategy as an interface, with a class for every way of choosing.

9
00:00:26.976 --> 00:00:29.339
Do we really need a class for each one?

10
00:00:30.433 --> 00:00:33.386
Let's start with the version most of us write.

11
00:00:33.386 --> 00:00:37.365
One method for the whole routine, with the choosing inside it.

12
00:00:37.365 --> 00:00:38.713
If cheapest, do this.

13
00:00:38.713 --> 00:00:40.317
Else if fastest, do that.

14
00:00:40.317 --> 00:00:42.243
Else if it's raining, the van.

15
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:46.924
Every new way to choose adds a branch, right in the middle of the routine.

16
00:00:46.924 --> 00:00:53.189
The weighing and the labels have nothing to do with it, and still, it's their method we edit every time.

17
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.057
Wouldn't it be nice to hand the shopkeeper today's rule, and leave the routine alone?

18
00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:00.416
Picture it like this.

19
00:01:00.416 --> 00:01:05.594
The routine hangs on a card behind the counter: weigh, choose, book, label.

20
00:01:05.594 --> 00:01:07.941
The choose step has an empty slot.

21
00:01:07.941 --> 00:01:12.083
Each morning, someone drops a small rule card into the slot.

22
00:01:12.083 --> 00:01:13.188
Cheapest, today.

23
00:01:13.188 --> 00:01:14.431
Tomorrow, fastest.

24
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:15.497
Let's map it.

25
00:01:15.497 --> 00:01:18.751
The routine card is the code that owns the steps.

26
00:01:18.751 --> 00:01:22.736
The small rule card is one decision, passed in from outside.

27
00:01:22.736 --> 00:01:27.119
And the routine only ever asks the rule card one kind of question.

28
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:30.303
Java's sort works exactly like this.

29
00:01:30.303 --> 00:01:33.328
It owns the routine for putting a list in order.

30
00:01:33.328 --> 00:01:37.236
It leaves one decision to you: of these two, which goes first?

31
00:01:37.236 --> 00:01:40.199
The object that answers is called a comparator.

32
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:46.455
Let's shrink it to three couriers, each with a price and a delivery time.

33
00:01:46.455 --> 00:01:48.875
Pedal Post: 4.50, and 30 minutes.

34
00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:51.516
Big Blue Vans: 3.50, and 90 minutes.

35
00:01:51.516 --> 00:01:55.697
And Kestrel, a new courier in town: 4.50, and 20 minutes.

36
00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.858
Sort them cheapest first.

37
00:01:57.858 --> 00:01:59.894
Big Blue Vans leads, easy.

38
00:01:59.894 --> 00:02:03.184
But Pedal Post and Kestrel both cost 4.50.

39
00:02:03.184 --> 00:02:04.515
Who comes second?

40
00:02:04.515 --> 00:02:05.768
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:12.354
The demo prints: Big Blue Vans, Pedal Post, Kestrel.

42
00:02:12.354 --> 00:02:16.915
If you guessed Kestrel, because K comes before P, that's a fair guess.

43
00:02:16.915 --> 00:02:17.892
And it's wrong.

44
00:02:17.892 --> 00:02:20.172
The sort never looked at the names.

45
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.691
Pedal Post was ahead of Kestrel in the list, so it stays ahead.

46
00:02:24.691 --> 00:02:29.458
The JDK's sort is stable: it never swaps two items that compare equal.

47
00:02:29.458 --> 00:02:31.705
A tie keeps the order it came in.

48
00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:33.578
Now swap the rule card.

49
00:02:33.578 --> 00:02:37.152
Fastest first: Kestrel, Pedal Post, Big Blue Vans.

50
00:02:37.152 --> 00:02:40.155
And one more card: cheapest, then fastest.

51
00:02:40.155 --> 00:02:43.087
Price first, and only on a tie, the time.

52
00:02:43.087 --> 00:02:46.089
Kestrel's 20 minutes beat Pedal Post's 30.

53
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:53.966
So the three lines from the lesson's StrategyDemo read: cheapest, fastest, and cheapest then fastest.

54
00:02:53.966 --> 00:02:56.656
Same list, same sort, three rule cards.

55
00:02:57.567 --> 00:02:58.649
Here's the code.

56
00:02:58.649 --> 00:03:00.882
Each rule card is one expression.

57
00:03:00.882 --> 00:03:05.955
comparingLong takes a function that pulls out the sort key, here the price.

58
00:03:05.955 --> 00:03:10.623
thenComparingLong chains a second rule behind it, used only on a tie.

59
00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:13.600
In Kotlin, compareBy and thenBy do the same.

60
00:03:13.833 --> 00:03:18.547
And the routine never changes: sort the list with whatever card you're handed.

61
00:03:18.547 --> 00:03:21.207
Three ways to choose, and not one new class.

62
00:03:22.133 --> 00:03:26.605
Nobody writes a class called PriceThenTimeComparator any more.

63
00:03:26.605 --> 00:03:28.120
It had a long career.

64
00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.129
Let it retire.

65
00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:32.849
Jokes aside, that's the real answer to our question.

66
00:03:32.849 --> 00:03:36.770
Since Java 8, a lambda or a method reference is the class.

67
00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:42.111
In Kotlin, any function from a parcel to a price already is a pricing strategy.

68
00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:43.975
Now the names.

69
00:03:43.975 --> 00:03:50.367
The swappable decision is called a strategy: one interchangeable rule, passed in by the caller.

70
00:03:50.367 --> 00:03:54.135
The code that uses it, sort here, is called the context.

71
00:03:54.135 --> 00:03:56.692
Together, that's the Strategy pattern.

72
00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.640
Here the counter picture breaks.

73
00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:03.019
A shopkeeper remembers that yesterday's van was late.

74
00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:05.506
A strategy shouldn't remember anything.

75
00:04:05.506 --> 00:04:13.029
A lambda that changes a field each time it runs is a strategy with hidden memory: a shopkeeper still holding a grudge.

76
00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:17.767
And two threads sorting with it at once will corrupt each other's results.

77
00:04:17.767 --> 00:04:20.095
So keep it pure: input in, answer out.

78
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.589
The JDK hands you strategies in more places than sorting.

79
00:04:24.589 --> 00:04:31.767
A thread pool takes a handler for a full queue, and groupingBy takes the function that picks each element's group.

80
00:04:31.767 --> 00:04:35.356
The library owns the loop, and asks you for one decision.

81
00:04:36.267 --> 00:04:37.353
So, three rules.

82
00:04:37.353 --> 00:04:39.118
One: keep a strategy pure.

83
00:04:39.118 --> 00:04:46.449
Two: when the decision needs more than one method, like book and track, write a real interface with classes.

84
00:04:46.449 --> 00:04:54.256
Three: even for one method, a named one-line interface, like PriceStrategy, can say more than a bare function type.

85
00:04:55.167 --> 00:04:58.418
So, do we need a class for every way of choosing?

86
00:04:58.418 --> 00:04:58.617
No.

87
00:04:58.617 --> 00:05:02.398
The routine stays put, and the one decision is passed in.

88
00:05:02.398 --> 00:05:06.777
Read it out loud: sort the couriers, cheapest first, then fastest.

89
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:07.844
Quick check.

90
00:05:07.844 --> 00:05:13.819
A fourth courier is added at the end of the list: Gull Express, 3.50, and 60 minutes.

91
00:05:13.819 --> 00:05:16.983
With cheapest, then fastest, who comes first?

92
00:05:20.167 --> 00:05:21.142
Gull Express.

93
00:05:21.142 --> 00:05:25.345
It ties Big Blue Vans on price, and 60 minutes beats 90.

94
00:05:25.345 --> 00:05:30.299
The whole order: Gull Express, Big Blue Vans, Kestrel, Pedal Post.
